  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Red Mountain began as a tiny mining camp called Osdick, founded after prospector Pete Osdick struck silver in 1919.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        3,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://desertgazette.com/blog/red-mountain-california/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The post office opened in 1922 under the name Osdick, putting the settlement on the map before it grew into a boomtown.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://desertgazette.com/blog/red-mountain-california/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "During the 1920s and 1930s, Red Mountain boomed alongside the Kelly Silver Mine, one of California’s richest silver producers at the time.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://desertgazette.com/blog/red-mountain-california/",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town’s rough charm included saloons like the Owl Café, where miners drank whiskey, gossiped, and danced until dawn.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://desertgazette.com/blog/red-mountain-california/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 2022, a $1.5 million grant helped clean arsenic‑laden sediment from historic gold and silver mining at the Kelly Mine complex.",
